      "id": "GENUS_AEGITHALOS",
      "name": "Aegithalos",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Tiny tail-heavy birds that move through woodland in chattering flocks. They build one of the most elaborate nests in European ornithology — an oval elastic cocoon of moss and lichen woven with spider silk and lined with hundreds of feathers, expandable as the chicks grow. In hard winters they huddle together for warmth at roost.",
      "lineage": "Long-tailed Tits",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "AEGITHALOS_CAUDATUS",
          "name": "Aegithalos caudatus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Long-tailed tit",
          "lineage": "Long-tailed Tits",
          "continents": [
            "Europe",
            "Asia"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 18,
          "description": "The long-tailed tit or long-tailed bushtit is a common bird species belonging to the bushtit family found throughout Eurasia. An insectivore, it inhabits deciduous and mixed woodlands as well as scrub, heathland, farmland, parks and gardens. The plumage of this small long-tailed bird is predominantly black and white with varying amounts of pink and grey.",
          "sourcedFrom": "wikipedia",
